# `append acc (slice cs a b)` is fused: the range is copied straight out of
# `cs` and the view the slice would have been is never built. Since a range
# past the end answers none (#1890), a program dispatches on a slice before it
# appends it, and the fused pair is written only by the standard library,
# whose ranges come from a scan of the same bytes. std/json is where it lives:
# the run of plain bytes before each escape in a string is appended that way.
# Forty escapes in one string, so a view per run would show here as forty
# allocations. With the emitter's fusion removed this file reads allocs=54 and
# sh_bytes=1056 against the 12 and 48 below.
import "std/json"

fn escaped acc 0
  acc

fn escaped acc n
  escaped "{acc}cde\\n" (n - 1)

pub play = print (length (json/decode "\"{escaped "" 40}\""))
